Returns `stale = true` if the object no longer exists.

#### Queries

A query picks tables by their string keys.

<table><thead><tr><th width="317.2667236328125">Query</th><th>Matches</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>"Ammo"</code></td><td>Tables with a non nil <mark style="color:violet;"><code>Ammo</code></mark></td></tr><tr><td><code>{ "Ammo", "MaxAmmo" }</code></td><td>Tables with both keys</td></tr><tr><td><code>{ "Ammo", Team = "Red" }</code></td><td><mark style="color:violet;"><code>Ammo</code></mark> set and <code>Team == "Red"</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>{ Owner = t }</code></td><td><mark style="color:violet;"><code>Owner</code></mark> points at that exact gc table</td></tr><tr><td><code>function(t) return t.Ammo > 10 end</code></td><td>Any table the function accepts, it gets a snapshot</td></tr></tbody></table>

Key queries are matched natively and are fast. Function queries call lua for every table, so use keys and narrow the rest with <mark style="color:blue;">`filter`</mark>.

Searches do not block other scripts while they run.

#### Gc tables

Results are gc tables, real lua tables that read and write the game table live.

```lua
local gun = gc.first({ "Ammo", "MaxAmmo" })

print(gun.Ammo) -- reads the game table
gun.Ammo = 999 -- writes it, errors if the key does not exist
print(gun.Stats.Kills) -- nested tables are gc tables too

for k, v in pairs(gun) do
    print(k, v)
end

print(gc.address(gun), tostring(gun)) -- 2d00000  GcTable: 0x2d00000
```

The same address always gives the same gc table, so they work as keys in your own tables. Every function that takes an address also takes a gc table.

#### Query options

<table><thead><tr><th width="115.199951171875">Option</th><th width="76.5">Default</th><th>Description</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>limit</td><td><mark style="color:green;"><code>0</code></mark></td><td>Stop after N tables. A number in place of the options table does the same</td></tr><tr><td>filter</td><td>.</td><td><code>function(snapshot)</code> run after the key match</td></tr><tr><td>sizes</td><td>.</td><td>Only tables whose <mark style="color:violet;"><code>lsizenode</code></mark> is in this list</td></tr><tr><td>exhaustive</td><td><mark style="color:green;"><code>false</code></mark></td><td>Skip the size cache and search every string size</td></tr><tr><td>budget_ms</td><td><mark style="color:green;"><code>0</code></mark></td><td>Give up after N milliseconds. <mark style="color:violet;"><code>0</code></mark> is unlimited</td></tr><tr><td>all_vms</td><td><mark style="color:green;"><code>true</code></mark></td><td>Try the other lua VMs when the selected one has no match, and switch to the one that does</td></tr></tbody></table>

<mark style="color:purple;">`gc`</mark> `find(`<mark style="color:green;">`query`</mark> `[,` <mark style="color:green;">`options`</mark>`])` -> <mark style="color:purple;">`table`</mark>\
Every matching table.

```lua
gc.find("Ammo")
gc.find({ "Ammo" }, 5)
gc.find({ "Health" }, { filter = function(t) return t.Health < 50 end })
```

<mark style="color:purple;">`gc`</mark> `first(`<mark style="color:green;">`query`</mark> `[,` <mark style="color:green;">`options`</mark>`])` -> <mark style="color:purple;">`table?`</mark>\
First matching table, or `nil`.

```lua
local sword = gc.first({ Name = "Sword", "Damage" })
```

<mark style="color:purple;">`gc`</mark> `read(`<mark style="color:green;">`query`</mark> `[,` <mark style="color:green;">`field`</mark> `[,` <mark style="color:green;">`options`</mark>`]])` -> <mark style="color:purple;">`table`</mark>\
Values from every matching table. `field` is a key, a list of keys, or `nil` for full snapshots.

```lua
gc.read("Ammo", "Ammo") -- { 30, 12, 999 }
gc.read("Ammo", { "Ammo", "MaxAmmo" }) -- { { Ammo = 30, MaxAmmo = 30 }, ... }
gc.read("Ammo") -- { snapshot, ... }
```

<mark style="color:purple;">`gc`</mark> `write(`<mark style="color:green;">`query`</mark>, <mark style="color:green;">`fields`</mark> `[,` <mark style="color:green;">`options`</mark>`])` -> <mark style="color:purple;">`number`</mark>\
Writes into every matching table and returns how many values changed. Only existing keys are written, and only when the value differs. `fields` can be a function that gets a snapshot and returns the fields.

```lua
gc.write({ "Ammo", "MaxAmmo" }, { Ammo = 999 })
gc.write("Ammo", function(t) return { Ammo = t.MaxAmmo } end)
```

<mark style="color:purple;">`gc`</mark> `lock(`<mark style="color:green;">`query`</mark>, <mark style="color:green;">`fields`</mark> `[,` <mark style="color:green;">`options`</mark>`])` -> <mark style="color:purple;">`lock`</mark>\
Keeps writing `fields` into every matching table in the background. Runs on its own thread, so it keeps going while your script waits.

```lua
local lock = gc.lock({ "Ammo", "MaxAmmo" }, { Ammo = 999 })

print(lock:count(), lock:writes())
lock:stop(true)     -- true puts the old values back
```

| Option       | Default                                  | Description                          |
| ------------ | ---------------------------------------- | ------------------------------------ |
| interval\_ms | <mark style="color:green;">`15`</mark>   | Time between writes                  |
| refind\_ms   | <mark style="color:green;">`5000`</mark> | Time between searches for new tables |

The query options work here too.

| Method                 | Returns                                                 |
| ---------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------- |
| `lock:stop([restore])` | Stops the lock. `true` restores the values it overwrote |
| `lock:count()`         | Tables currently locked                                 |
| `lock:writes()`        | Total writes made                                       |
| `lock:running()`       | <mark style="color:purple;">`bool`</mark>               |
| `lock:refind()`        | Searches again right away                               |
| `lock:targets()`       | The locked gc tables                                    |

A lock keeps its tables after it changes a value the query filtered on, so `gc.lock({ Ammo = 0 }, { Ammo = 999 })` works. Locks stop with their script.

<mark style="color:purple;">`gc`</mark> `snapshot(`<mark style="color:green;">`table`</mark> `[,` <mark style="color:green;">`options`</mark>`])` -> <mark style="color:purple;">`table?`</mark>\
A plain copy of a table. Nested tables stay gc tables unless `depth` copies them too.

```lua
local data = gc.snapshot(gun)
local deep = gc.snapshot(gun, { depth = 2, limit = 500 })
```

<table><thead><tr><th width="262.8333740234375">Value</th><th>Comes back as</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>string, number, boolean, vector</td><td>Itself</td></tr><tr><td>table</td><td>Gc table</td></tr><tr><td>function, userdata, thread</td><td><code>{</code> <mark style="color:violet;"><code>type</code></mark>, <mark style="color:violet;"><code>address</code></mark>, <mark style="color:violet;"><code>address_hex</code></mark> <code>}</code></td></tr></tbody></table>

#### Writing values

`t.key = value`, `gc.update`, `gc.write`, `gc.lock` and `setgc` accept <mark style="color:green;">`number`</mark>, <mark style="color:green;">`boolean`</mark>, <mark style="color:green;">`nil`</mark>, <mark style="color:green;">`vector3`</mark>, <mark style="color:green;">`string`</mark> and <mark style="color:purple;">gc</mark> tables.

* Only existing keys can be written, new keys cannot be added.
* Strings must already exist in the game, and gc tables must be from the same lua VM.
* Numbers and booleans are safe. Strings and tables can be freed by the collector while the game still uses them, which crashes.

<mark style="color:purple;">`gc`</mark> `write(`<mark style="color:green;">`address`</mark>, <mark style="color:green;">`offset`</mark>, <mark style="color:green;">`kind`</mark>, <mark style="color:green;">`value`</mark>`)` -> <mark style="color:purple;">`bool`</mark>\
Raw typed write, for fields this module does not model. `kind` is one of (<mark style="color:blue;">`u8`</mark>, <mark style="color:blue;">`u16`</mark>, <mark style="color:blue;">`u32`</mark>, <mark style="color:blue;">`i32`</mark>, <mark style="color:blue;">`u64`</mark>, <mark style="color:blue;">`ptr`</mark>, <mark style="color:blue;">`float`</mark>, <mark style="color:blue;">`double`</mark>). Also available as `gc.write_raw`.

```lua
gc.write(address, 0x04, "u8", 1)
```

No validation beyond bounds. A wrong offset corrupts the object leading to a crash.

